## 1. Aim

To implement a `Text` class using dynamic memory allocation and overload assignment, addition, comparison and output operators.

## 2. C++ Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>
using namespace std;

class Text {
    char* data;
    static int copies;

public:
    // Constructor
    Text(const char* s = "") {
        data = new char[strlen(s) + 1];
        strcpy(data, s);
    }

    // Copy constructor
    Text(const Text& t) {
        data = new char[strlen(t.data) + 1];
        strcpy(data, t.data);
        copies++;
        cout << "Copy constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // Copy assignment operator
    Text& operator=(const Text& t) {
        if (this != &t) {
            char* temp = new char[strlen(t.data) + 1];
            strcpy(temp, t.data);

            delete[] data;
            data = temp;
        }
        return *this;
    }

    // Addition operator
    Text operator+(const Text& t) const {
        int len = strlen(data) + strlen(t.data);
        char* temp = new char[len + 1];

        strcpy(temp, data);
        strcat(temp, t.data);

        Text result(temp);
        delete[] temp;

        return result;
    }

    // Equality operator
    bool operator==(const Text& t) const {
        return strcmp(data, t.data) == 0;
    }

    // Not equal operator
    bool operator!=(const Text& t) const {
        return !(*this == t);
    }

    // Output operator
    friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& out, const Text& t) {
        out << t.data;
        return out;
    }

    // Destructor
    ~Text() {
        delete[] data;
    }

    static int getCopies() {
        return copies;
    }
};

int Text::copies = 0;

int main() {
    Text a("Hello");
    Text b(" World");
    Text c("!");

    // Concatenation
    Text result = a + b + c;
    cout << "Combined text: " << result << endl;

    // Comparison
    Text x("Hello");
    Text y("Hello");

    cout << boolalpha;
    cout << "Equal: " << (x == y) << endl;
    cout << "Not equal: " << (x != y) << endl;

    // Assignment
    Text d;
    d = a;
    cout << "Assigned text: " << d << endl;

    // Self-assignment
    d = d;
    cout << "After self-assignment: " << d << endl;

    // Empty message
    Text empty("");
    cout << "Empty message: [" << empty << "]" << endl;

    // Long message
    Text longText("This is a long SMS message for testing.");
    cout << "Long message: " << longText << endl;

    cout << "Total copies: " << Text::getCopies() << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## 3. Expected Output

```text
Combined text: Hello World!
Equal: true
Not equal: false
Assigned text: Hello
After self-assignment: Hello
Empty message: []
Long message: This is a long SMS message for testing.
Total copies: 0
```

*Note: With this implementation and C++17, the compiler can eliminate unnecessary copies when evaluating `a + b + c`. The copy count may differ if compiler optimizations or return-value elision differ.*

## 4. Design and Analysis

### A. Operators that cannot be overloaded

| Operator | Reason |
|---|---|
| `::` | Scope resolution |
| `.` | Member access |
| `.*` | Pointer-to-member access |
| `?:` | Conditional operator |
| `sizeof` | Compile-time size calculation |

These operators have fixed meanings in C++.

### B. Rules of Operator Overloading

1. Operator precedence cannot be changed.
2. Operator associativity cannot be changed.
3. Number of operands (arity) cannot be changed.
4. At least one operand must be a user-defined type.

### C. Why must operator= be a member function?

- The assignment operator must be a member function.
- It modifies the current object.
- It can access private data members directly.

### D. When should an operator be a friend?

A friend function is useful when an operator needs access to private members but the left operand is not the class object.

Example:

```cpp
friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& out, const Text& t);
```

The left operand is `cout`, so `<<` is implemented as a non-member friend function.

