## 7. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

### A. Using std::string

Instead of a dynamically allocated character array, we can use `std::string`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string a = "Hello";
    string b = " World";
    string c = "!";

    string result = a + b + c;

    cout << result << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

**Output:**

```text
Hello World!
```

`std::string` automatically manages memory and provides built-in operators.

### B. Using += on a Copy

Another way to implement addition is:

```cpp
Text operator+(Text a, const Text& b) {
    // Append b to a and return a
}
```

Here, the first argument is passed by value, so a copy is made.

### C. Complexity Analysis

Let the lengths of the three messages be n1, n2 and n3.

| Operation | Time Complexity | Space Complexity |
|---|---|---|
| Copy constructor | O(n) | O(n) |
| Assignment | O(n) | O(n) |
| Comparison | O(n) | O(1) |
| Concatenation | O(n1 + n2) | O(n1 + n2) |

For `a + b + c`:

- First concatenation copies n1 + n2 characters.
- Second concatenation copies n1 + n2 + n3 characters.
- Total character copying is approximately 2n1 + 2n2 + n3.
- Temporary memory is required for intermediate results.

### D. Comparison

| Text Class | std::string |
|---|---|
| Manual memory management | Automatic memory management |
| Requires copy constructor | Built-in copying |
| Requires destructor | No manual destructor |
| More control over memory | Easier to use |
| More code | Less code |

Both approaches have O(n) copying complexity for a message of length n.

## 8. Final Conclusion

Writing the Text class helps us understand dynamic memory allocation, deep copying, operator overloading and memory management. Although `std::string` is easier and safer for normal applications, a custom Text class is useful when special memory management or specific behaviour is required.
